# Anti-Oracle Leakage

Benchmark code must not give the system under test the answers it will later be graded against.

## Hard Rule

Model-facing prompts, extraction runners, repair loops, and tool dispatch inputs MUST NOT consume benchmark gold or grader internals while generating the artifact being graded.

Forbidden examples:

- Passing `expected_events`, `expected_anchored_spans`, `anchor_words`, expected slugs, expected terms, or expected line ranges into a prompt or repair model unless those fields are part of the real user/corpus input.
- Importing private grader helpers (for example `_events_match`, `_collect_*`) into a runner or repair step to decide whether generated output should be accepted.
- Adding static prompt examples that reuse high-signal terms from the active benchmark gold (NPC names, spell/item names, place names, anchor words).
- Editing gold after seeing model output unless the rationale is corpus/design based and documented.

Allowed examples:

- Graders reading gold to evaluate completed outputs.
- Reports/telemetry including gold-derived pass/fail diagnostics after generation is complete.
- Prompts containing generic instructions and synthetic examples that do not overlap active benchmark answers.
- Repair loops using only source corpus text plus the candidate artifact being repaired.

## Required Checks

Before claiming a benchmark improvement:

- Confirm the model-facing prompt path does not include gold-only terms or expected outputs.
- Confirm repair/mutation code is gold-agnostic and does not call grader internals.
- Add a regression test for any oracle leak you just removed.
- If gold changes, document a corpus/design rationale in the gold file or adjacent README; never justify with model behavior.

If removing leakage drops pass rate, keep the honest red result and fix the capability, prompt, schema, or corpus grounding instead of routing around the failure.
